From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Mon, 21 Nov 94 20:11:21 WET
Subject: [#94-11-305] Wavelets, Image compression

Phil G. Fraering writes:
> 
> I think there are some misconceptions going around here about
> image compression formats.
> 
> MPEG is a standard, but the difference between mpeg1 and mpeg2,
> as far as I could tell from reading the compression faq's, isn't
> in the algorithm itself but in the volume and quality of the
> data being compressed. MPEG2 is, AFAIK, the same as MPEG1. Changing
> from one to the other isn't going to help image quality much.

  Actually, MPEG-2 does a lot more than MPEG-1 from what I've read. First
of all, MPEG-2 is optimized better for interlaced images which most TV 
signals are. If DirectTV isn't using this, that could be one of
their problems. Secondly, MPEG-2 specifies LayerI,II, and III audio
compression, and finally, it specifies the way the video streams
should be encoded for best playback. MPEG-2 also is scalable
to larger signals, so for instance, MPEG-2 could use the full
chroma and luma bandwidth for encoding instead of decimating
chroma by 4 and luma by a factor of 2 like MPEG-1 did.

  No doubt, MPEG has artifacts, but from MPEG video I've seen, they
aren't as bad as the one's Tim experienced. The one thing that irritates
me about both MPEG and JPEG is the "blockiness".  Perhaps JPEG/MPEG
could take some lessons from the Wavelet people and encode the image
using several block sizes: 8x8 DCT, 16x16 DCT, ... etc.


   Wavelets aren't going to take hold until someone makes a free, fast,
wavelet compression utility portable to all platforms, and the
people in alt.binaries.pictures.erotica start using it. :)

-Ray



