Subject: [#94-11-307] Millenial Fever

BTW, I consider the Foresight millenial fever to be such a
tragic piece of news, because I have considered the work
done by Drexler, Merkle et. al. on theoretical applied
science to be quite important.  Indeed it is perhaps the 
cornerstone of Extropian thought, as it gives us a rational basis 
for determining the probability of cryonics reanimation, uploading,
and several other far-future capabilities that radically improve
the choices we have available on how to live our lives now.

By predicting the Coming of the Lord in 2000 (or 2010,
or whatever), whether out of naive business hopes or
millenial fever, Foresight sets itself up for failure
and consequent ridicule.  Unfortuneately that repudiation,
while correctly applied to silly failed predictions (hasn't
Drexler learned from the O'Neill space-colonies-in-1990 idiocy?),
will also reflect badly on theoretical applied science, which is 
a solid and crucially important contribution that Drexler
and Merkle have made.

Millenial fever -- snap out of it!

Nick Szabo					szabo@netcom.com

