>chroma and luma bandwidth for encoding instead of decimating
>chroma by 4 and luma by a factor of 2 like MPEG-1 did.
>
I find it ironic that these popular compression schemes use just as many
bits to transmit 10 seconds of blackness as to transmit a complex moving
image. Same for sound. 10 seconds of silence takes just as many bits as
complex sounds. 

I realize that some mediums are not easily adapted to variable-rate signals
but I would guess that the compression efficency to be gained would make it
worthwhile in some cases. 

So I'm suggesting a compression scheme with peak bandwidth requirements
much greater than average bandwidth requirements. Peaks would occur during
complex image changes then smaller changes would use less bits. For
programs stored on CD ROM or hard disk the advantage would be immeadiate.
For broadcast signals the advantage would only be realized if many signals
are statistically multiplexed together and buffered at the receive end. 

Problem is I don't have any hard data on how much bandwidth could be saved
this way for typical and not-so-typical audio and video content. I suspect
a lot but I'd love to hear from those with more experience than I in this
area.


