From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Mon, 21 Nov 1994 17:23:12 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-11-313] NANOTECH: Drexler predicts when it will happen

With all due respect, I think Eric Drexler has gone off the deep
end.  Millenial fever is at hand, run for cover!

Seriously, I think anybody who works desinging or building actual,
rather than theoretical, mechanical or molecular scale technology for 
a living, taking a good look at today's tooling base, has to observe 
that the tooling needed to build diamandoid machines does not exist now, 
and will not be built any time within the planning horizon of any 
business or government.  Molecular self-assembly falls vastly short of 
assemblers in terms of capability and flexibility, and lithography is 
still several orders of magnitude too large.  There is simply no clearly 
discernable or easy path from here to there.  Nor has Drexler, or anybody 
else, ever presented a sufficiently detailed design for a self-reproducing 
machine.  The devil is in the last 1% of the details.  Drexler 
assemblers need to be 99.99% self-reproducing or better to be
economical, and much better still to perform some of the "exponential
growth" feats postulated for them.  There are many layers of Hell between 
here and assemblers.  It will take tens of decades, rather than tens of 
years, for diamondoid assemblers to make their appearance in the tooling 
noosphere.

We will, however, see continued, perhaps breathtaking progress
in lithography, agile manufacturing and biotechnology, which will give 
us many of the benefits that Drexlerian nanotech promises, much sooner.
Furthermore, Drexlerian nanotechnology is invaluable as a guide for
determining what kinds of technologies will be possible in the 22nd
century.  Postulating it as a near term or practical business 
proposition is idiocy.

